== “It Is God Who Justifies”

#quote[Rom 8:33.]

A #smallcaps[wonderful] thing, this being justified, or made just! If we had never broken the laws of God, we would not have needed it, for we would have been just in ourselves. The man who has all his life done the things he ought to have done, and has never done anything he ought not to have done, is justified by the law. But you, dear reader, are not of that sort, I am quite sure. You are too honest to pretend to be without sin, and therefore you need to be justified.

Now, if you justify yourself, you will simply be deceiving yourself. So do not try it. It is never worthwhile.

If you ask your fellow mortals to justify you, what can they do? You can get some of them to speak well of you for a penny, and others will speak ill of you behind your back for less than that. Their judgment is not worth much.

Our text says, “It is God who justifies,” and this is far more to the point. It is an astonishing fact, and one that we ought to consider with care. Come and see.

In the first place, #emph[nobody but God would ever have thought of justifying those who are guilty]. They have lived in open rebellion; they have done evil with both hands; they have gone from bad to worse; they have turned back to sin even after they have smarted for it and have therefore been forced to leave it for a while. They have broken the law and trampled on the gospel. They have refused proclamations of mercy and have persisted in ungodliness. How can they be forgiven and justified? Their fellow men, despairing of them, say, “They are hopeless cases.” Even Christians look on them with sorrow rather than with hope. But not so their God. He, in the splendor of his electing grace, having chosen some of them before the foundation of the world, will not rest till he has justified them and made them accepted in the Beloved. Is it not written, “Those He predestined, He also called; those He called, He also justified; those He justified, He also glorified”? So you see there are some whom the Lord resolves to justify: why should you and I not be among them?

No one but a God would ever have thought of justifying #emph[me]. I am a wonder to myself. I do not doubt that grace is seen just as clearly in others. Look at Saul of Tarsus, who foamed at the mouth against God’s servants. Like a hungry wolf, he tore into the lambs and the sheep right and left; and yet God struck him down on the road to Damascus, and changed his heart, and so fully justified him that before long this man became the greatest preacher of justification by faith who ever lived. He must often have marveled that he was justified by faith in Christ Jesus; for he was once a determined stickler for salvation by the works of the law. No one but God would ever have thought of justifying such a man as Saul the persecutor; but the Lord God is glorious in grace.

But even if anybody had thought of justifying the ungodly, #emph[no one but God could have done it]. It is quite impossible for anyone to forgive offenses that have not been committed against himself. Someone has greatly injured you; you can forgive him, and I hope you will; but no third person can forgive him apart from you. If the wrong is done to you, the pardon must come from you. If we have sinned against God, it is in God’s power to forgive; for the sin is against himself. That is why David says, in the fifty-first Psalm: “Against You, You only, have I sinned and done what is evil in Your sight”; for then God, against whom the offense is committed, can put the offense away. What we owe to God, our great Creator can cancel, if he pleases; and if he cancels it, it is canceled. Only the great God, against whom we have committed the sin, can blot out that sin; let us, therefore, make sure that we go to him and seek mercy at his hands. Let us not be led astray by priests, who would have us confess to them; they have no warrant in the Word of God for their claims. But even if they were ordained to pronounce absolution in God’s name, it must still be better to go ourselves to the great Lord through Jesus Christ, the Mediator, and seek and find pardon at his hand; since we are sure that this is the right way. Religion by proxy carries too great a risk: you had better see to your soul’s affairs yourself, and leave them in no man’s hands.

Only God can justify the ungodly; but #emph[he can do it to perfection]. He casts our sins behind his back; he blots them out; he says that though they are searched for, they will not be found. With no other reason for it but his own infinite goodness, he has prepared a glorious way by which he can make scarlet sins as white as snow, and remove our transgressions from us as far as the east is from the west. He says, “I will not remember your sins.” He goes so far as to make an end of sin. One of old cried out in amazement, “Who is a God like You, who pardons iniquity and passes over the transgression of the remnant of His inheritance? He does not retain His anger forever, because He delights in mercy.”

We are not now speaking of justice, nor of God’s dealing with men according to what they deserve. If you claim to deal with the righteous Lord on the terms of the law, everlasting wrath threatens you, for that is what you deserve. Blessed be his name, he has not dealt with us according to our sins; but now he deals with us on terms of free grace and infinite compassion, and he says, “I will receive you graciously, and love you freely.” Believe it, for it is certainly true that the great God is able to treat the guilty with abundant mercy; yes, he is able to treat the ungodly as if they had always been godly. Read carefully the parable of the prodigal son, and see how the forgiving father received the returning wanderer with as much love as if he had never gone away, and had never defiled himself with prostitutes. So far did he carry this that the elder brother began to grumble at it; but the father never withdrew his love. O my brother, however guilty you may be, if you will only come back to your God and Father, he will treat you as if you had never done wrong. He will regard you as just, and deal with you accordingly. What do you say to this?

Do you not see — for I want to bring out clearly what a splendid thing it is — that while no one but God would think of justifying the ungodly, and no one but God could do it, the Lord can do it? See how the apostle puts the challenge: “Who will bring any charge against God’s elect? It is God who justifies.” If God has justified a man, it is well done, it is rightly done, it is justly done, it is everlastingly done. I read the other day in a paper that is full of venom against the Gospel and those who preach it, that we hold some kind of theory by which we imagine that sin can be removed from men. We hold no theory; we proclaim a fact. The grandest fact under heaven is this — that Christ by his precious blood does actually put away sin, and that God, for Christ’s sake, dealing with men on terms of divine mercy, forgives the guilty and justifies them, not according to anything that he sees in them, or foresees will be in them, but according to the riches of his mercy which lie in his own heart. This we have preached, do preach, and will preach as long as we live. “It is God who justifies” — who justifies the ungodly; he is not ashamed of doing it, nor are we of preaching it.

The justification which comes from God himself must be beyond question. If the Judge acquits me, who can condemn me? If the highest court in the universe has pronounced me just, who will bring any charge against me? Justification from God is a sufficient answer to an awakened conscience. Through it the Holy Spirit breathes peace over our entire nature, and we are no longer afraid. With this justification we can answer all the roaring and reviling of Satan and ungodly men. With this we will be able to die: with this we will boldly rise again and face the last great judgment.

#quote[“Bold shall I stand in that great day, \ For who aught to my charge shall lay? \ While by my Lord absolved I am \ From sin’s tremendous curse and blame.”]

Friend, #emph[the Lord can blot out all your sins]. This is no shot in the dark. “Every sin and blasphemy will be forgiven men.” Though you are steeped up to your throat in crime, he can with a word remove the defilement, and say, “I am willing. Be clean!” The Lord is a great forgiver.

“I #smallcaps[believe in the forgiveness of sins].” Do #smallcaps[you]?

He can, this very hour, pronounce the sentence, “Your sins are forgiven; go in peace”; and if he does this, no power in heaven, or earth, or under the earth, can put you under suspicion, much less under wrath. Do not doubt the power of Almighty love. #emph[You] could not forgive your fellow man if he had offended you as you have offended God; but you must not measure God’s grain with your bushel; his thoughts and ways are as much above yours as the heavens are high above the earth.

“Well,” you say, “it would be a great miracle if the Lord were to pardon me.” Just so. It would be a supreme miracle, and therefore he is likely to do it; for he does “great and unsearchable things” that we did not expect.

I was myself struck down with a horrible sense of guilt, which made my life a misery to me; but when I heard the command, “Look to Me, and be saved, all the ends of the earth, for I am God, and there is no other” — I looked, and in a moment the Lord justified me. Jesus Christ, made sin for me, was what I saw, and that sight gave me rest. When those who had been bitten by the fiery serpents in the wilderness looked to the bronze serpent, they were healed at once; and so was I when I looked to the crucified Savior. The Holy Spirit, who enabled me to believe, gave me peace through believing. I felt as sure that I was forgiven as I had felt sure of condemnation before. I had been certain of my condemnation because the word of God declared it, and my conscience bore witness to it; but when the Lord justified me, I was made equally certain by the same witnesses. The word of the Lord in Scripture says, “He who believes in Him is not condemned,” and my conscience bears witness that I believed, and that God in pardoning me is just. So I have the witness of the Holy Spirit and of my own conscience, and these two agree in one. Oh, how I wish that my reader would receive the testimony of God on this matter, and then very soon he would also have the witness in himself!

I venture to say that a sinner justified by God stands on even surer footing than a righteous man justified by his works, if there is any such man. We could never be sure that we had done enough works; conscience would always be uneasy for fear that, after all, we might fall short, and we could rely only on the trembling verdict of a fallible judgment; but when God himself justifies, and the Holy Spirit bears witness to it by giving us peace with God, why, then we feel that the matter is sure and settled, and we enter into rest. No tongue can tell the depth of that calm which comes over the soul that has received the peace of God which surpasses all understanding. #emph[Friend, do seek it] #smallcaps[at once].
